# CUPS (Configuration and Update Server) — optional

CUPS lets The Things Stack push channel-plan / firmware updates to the
gateway. For a single home gateway this is overkill — stick to LNS only
(`tc.uri` + `tc.key` + `tc.trust`).

If you want CUPS anyway, on the gateway:

```bash
sudo cp /opt/linklabs/station/cups.uri.example /opt/linklabs/station/cups.uri
sudo cp /opt/linklabs/station/cups.key.example /opt/linklabs/station/cups.key
sudo cp /opt/linklabs/station/tc.trust          /opt/linklabs/station/cups.trust
sudo nano /opt/linklabs/station/cups.key   # paste real CUPS key
sudo chmod 600 /opt/linklabs/station/cups.key
sudo systemctl restart linklabs
```

When both `cups.*` and `tc.*` are present, Basic Station uses CUPS at
boot to (re)fetch the LNS credentials, then connects to LNS as usual.

Generate the CUPS key in TTN console with the
`Link as Gateway to a Gateway Server for traffic exchange` permission
plus `View gateway status` (and optionally `Edit basic gateway settings`
if you want CUPS to be able to overwrite the local config).
